s are studying the potential benefits and drawbacks of A.I. for students an=
d teachers.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color=
:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Iceland started a pilot program last fall in w=
hich teachers used chatbots like Claude, from Anthropic, to help brainstorm=
 ideas for new lessons, create learning games and help explain complex conc=
epts to students.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif=
;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In Sierra Leone, Google recently conduct=
ed an eight-week study with local schools to test whether its =E2=80=9Cguid=
ed learning=E2=80=9D A.I. system could help students with math. The company=
 reported that its short study had found modest gains. Google also noted th=
at students who already had strong math skills benefited more from using A.=
I. than students who were struggling.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17p=
x/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Estonia, one of the =
first countries to provide computers to students in schools, has embarked o=
n a more comprehensive program. Last year, researchers and education leader=
s there worked with OpenAI to develop a customized chatbot in Estonian that=
 is designed to ask students questions and push them to think for themselve=
s, rather than do students=E2=80=99 work for them.</span></p><p style=3D"fo=
nt:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=
=9CThe idea is to create guardrails to make sure that students are not losi=
ng their cognitive skills in the age of A.I.,=E2=80=9D Kristina Kallas, Est=
onia=E2=80=99s minister of education and research, told me.</span></p><p st=
yle=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><spa=
n>Countries and school districts that are incorporating A.I. into classroom=
